---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { gccRegion } from '../../regions/gcc';

const byCountry = gccRegion.countries.map((c) => ({
  ...c,
  ports: gccRegion.ports.filter((p) => p.country === c.slug),
}));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="GCC ports for China-origin freight"
  description="Port profiles for China-to-GCC freight: Jebel Ali, Jeddah, Dammam, Hamad, Sohar, Shuwaikh, Salalah and the inland dry ports, with what each one changes."
>
  <h1>GCC ports</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A port choice is not a preference; it is a decision about inland distance, congestion, free-zone
    status and which carrier services call there. The profiles below cover the gateways that actually
    handle China-origin cargo into the six GCC markets, including the inland dry ports that sit behind
    them.
  </p>

  <figure class="drawing-figure">
    <img src="/images/map-gcc-ports.png" alt="GCC port system map: Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar, the UAE, Saudi Arabia and Oman, with seaports marked and Riyadh dry port inland." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Drawing 07 — The GCC port system. Base map Natural Earth 1:10m; port positions are approximate, not for navigation.</figcaption>
  </figure>

<h2>How to choose a gateway</h2>
  <p>
    Four variables decide most of it. The first is the final destination: a container bound for Riyadh
    that lands in Jeddah still has a long inland leg, while Dammam shortens it for the Eastern
    Province. The second is congestion and berth waiting, which moves with season and service changes
    rather than with the port's brochure. The third is whether the consignee needs a free zone or a
    mainland clearance. The fourth is certification and inspection capacity — a port that cannot
    complete the required conformity check quickly turns a cheap freight rate into demurrage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where two ports are genuinely interchangeable, the comparison pages set out the trade-off rather
    than declaring a winner: <a href="/compare/jebel-ali-vs-khalifa/">Jebel Ali vs Khalifa</a>,
    <a href="/compare/jeddah-vs-dammam/">Jeddah vs Dammam</a>, and
    <a href="/compare/jebel-ali-vs-direct/">Jebel Ali vs direct GCC calls</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Ports by market</h2>
  {byCountry.map((c) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{c.name} <span class="meta">· {c.currency} · {c.vat === 0 ? 'no VAT' : `${Math.round(c.vat * 100)}% VAT`}</span></h3>
      <ul>
        {c.ports.map((p) => (
          <li><a href={`/ports/${p.slug}/`}>{p.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>What a port profile contains</h2>
  <p>
    Each profile covers the berths and terminal operators, the services that connect it to Chinese
    ports, the inland connections, the customs and inspection regime at that gate, and the failure
    modes that show up in practice — rolled cargo, congested berths, or a free zone that does not
    permit the re-export the buyer assumed. Figures are given as ranges where the source is a
    published tariff and as estimates where it is not; the page says which.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
